From 662dddd72f98418d751a821b6ce289a3859f28b7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pierre-Yves Le Borgne <pylaterreur@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 08:17:31 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Read up to 256 KiB per recv() on the TCP data path, encrypt
 as several AEAD chunks

SOCKET_BUF_SIZE (16383) is the largest AEAD chunk payload the protocol
allows, but the code also used it as the size of every recv(), so a relay
moved bulk data in 16 KiB steps: one recv, one encrypt or decrypt pass and
one send per chunk, plus a second recv whenever a full chunk (16417 bytes
on the wire) did not fit in the 16383-byte read.

aead_encrypt() now splits its input into as many chunks of at most
CHUNK_SIZE_MASK bytes as needed (aead_decrypt() already handled several
chunks per call), so the wire format is unchanged and old and new peers
interoperate. A connection starts reading 16383 bytes at a time and
multiplies the read size by 4 each time a recv() fills the buffer, up to
TCP_IO_MAX (256 KiB), so memory is only spent on connections that move
bulk data. -DTCP_IO_MAX=16383 restores the old behaviour.

The buffers that grew are given back by a timer (every TCP_IO_SWEEP, 5 s,
in ss-local, ss-redir, ss-server and ss-tunnel) when they are empty and
were not used since its previous run, so a connection that moved bulk
data and then idles does not keep them. For that, the first send in the
recv callbacks now resets the buffer when everything was sent, as the
other send paths already do, instead of leaving the length of the last
recv() in it.

Applies to ss-redir, ss-server, ss-tunnel and ss-local (data path only,
the SOCKS5 handshake stays at one chunk).
